# =============================================================================
# MIRAGE-DoH Reproducibility Artifact — .gitignore
# =============================================================================
#
# What is excluded, and why:
#
#   - Python caches and compiled bytecode: regenerated automatically.
#   - Virtual environments: reproducible from requirements.txt /
#     environment.yml; a reviewer should never clone a venv.
#   - The 32 GB datasets (CIC-DoH2023, Gen-C-DDD-2022): not redistributable
#     within a 1 GB GitHub repo; fetched by data/download_datasets.py.
#     The empty directory placeholders are kept via .gitkeep.
#   - Training outputs (outputs/): regenerated by the reproduce scripts; the
#     thesis numbers live in expected_outputs/ (committed) and are compared
#     against fresh runs, so a stale outputs/ directory would be misleading.
#   - Model checkpoints (*.pt, *.pth, *.ckpt, *.h5, *.onnx): not
#     redistributable in practice, and reproducible from configs/*.yaml.
#   - IDE and OS files: not part of the artifact; keep diffs readable.
#   - Jupyter checkpoints: not part of the artifact; the notebook workflow
#     is documented in README.md §3 but notebooks themselves are not used
#     by the artifact (the scripts are the entry point).
#
# What is explicitly NOT excluded (committed to the repository):
#
#   - expected_outputs/*.json, *.csv, *.dot: the thesis numbers.
#   - data/toy_dataset/*.csv: the 1 MB subset for the 2-minute demo.
#   - configs/*.yaml: hyperparameters (Equations 1-31).
#   - Everything under src/, scripts/, tests/, figures_src/.
#   - README.md, REPRODUCIBILITY_CHECKLIST.md, LICENSE, requirements.txt,
#     environment.yml, Dockerfile, setup.py.
#
# Recovery: what to do after a clone if you need an excluded file
#
#   data/raw/           -> python data/download_datasets.py
#   data/processed/     -> python data/preprocess_*.py (after download)
#   outputs/            -> run any script under scripts/reproduce_*
#   *.pt / *.pth        -> run scripts/train_mirage.py (regenerates them)
#   .env                -> not used by this artifact; documented here so a
#                          reviewer does not add one by mistake (there is no
#                          .env, .env.example, or secrets file in this
#                          repository, by design; see REPRODUCIBILITY_CHECKLIST
#                          §9 on the threat model)
# =============================================================================


# =============================================================================
# 1. Python
# =============================================================================

# Bytecode caches (regenerated by CPython on import).
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class

# Distribution / packaging artefacts (regenerated by `pip install`).
build/
develop-eggs/
dist/
downloads/
eggs/
.eggs/
lib/
lib64/
parts/
sdist/
var/
wheels/
share/python-wheels/
*.egg-info/
.installed.cfg
*.egg
MANIFEST

# PyInstaller (not used, but harmless to ignore if a reviewer experiments).
*.manifest
*.spec

# Unit test / coverage caches.
htmlcov/
.tox/
.nox/
.coverage
.coverage.*
.cache
nosetests.xml
coverage.xml
*.cover
*.py,cover
.hypothesis/
.pytest_cache/
pytestdebug.log

# Type-checker caches.
.mypy_cache/
.dmypy.json
dmypy.json
.pytype/
cython_debug/

# IPython / Jupyter checkpoints (the artifact is script-based; this is
# defensive, in case a reviewer opens a script in Jupyter).
.ipynb_checkpoints/
.ipython/
profile_default/


# =============================================================================
# 2. Virtual environments
# =============================================================================
# A reviewer should rebuild from requirements.txt or environment.yml;
# cloning a venv across platforms is known to fail. The conda env is
# recreated with `conda env create -f environment.yml`.

.venv/
venv/
ENV/
env/
env.bak/
venv.bak/


# =============================================================================
# 3. Datasets
# =============================================================================
# The full corpora are NOT committed. See README.md §6 and
# data/README_data.md for the official sources and SHA-256 checksums.
#
# The empty directory placeholders are kept via .gitkeep (below). A fresh
# clone has data/raw/ and data/processed/ ready for the download script.

data/raw/*
!data/raw/.gitkeep
data/processed/*
!data/processed/.gitkeep

# Common dataset archive formats (in case a reviewer drops an archive in
# the repo by mistake; a warning in the commit log is preferable to a
# 20 GB commit).
data/raw/*.zip
data/raw/*.tar
data/raw/*.tar.gz
data/raw/*.tgz
data/raw/*.7z
data/raw/*.rar
data/processed/*.zip
data/processed/*.tar
data/processed/*.tar.gz
data/processed/*.tgz

# The toy dataset IS committed (it is 1 MB and it powers the 2-minute
# demo). Explicitly un-ignore it so that no earlier rule catches it.
!data/toy_dataset/
!data/toy_dataset/*.csv
!data/toy_dataset/README.md
!data/toy_dataset/CHECKSUMS.txt


# =============================================================================
# 4. Training outputs
# =============================================================================
# outputs/ is where reproduce scripts write fresh runs. Those runs are
# compared against expected_outputs/ (committed) by
# expected_outputs/verification_script.py. A stale outputs/ would be
# misleading, so it is excluded.

outputs/
outputs/*
!outputs/.gitkeep

# Aggregated logs written by reproduce_all.sh.
*.log
logs/


# =============================================================================
# 5. Model checkpoints
# =============================================================================
# Checkpoints are large and reproducible from configs/*.yaml. A reviewer
# who needs them runs scripts/train_mirage.py, which writes them to
# outputs/checkpoints/ (also gitignored above).

*.pt
*.pth
*.ckpt
*.h5
*.hdf5
*.onnx
*.pb
*.tflite
*.safetensors
*.bin
*.model

# Torch optimiser states and compiled artefacts.
*.pkl
*.joblib
*.t7
*.npy
*.npz


# =============================================================================
# 6. IDE and editor files
# =============================================================================
# Not part of the artifact. Keeping them out of history keeps diffs
# readable for a reviewer who reads commit history.

.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*.swo
*~
*.sublime-workspace
*.sublime-project
.project
.classpath
.settings/
.idea_modules/
*.iml


# =============================================================================
# 7. OS files
# =============================================================================

# macOS
.DS_Store
.DS_Store?
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes
Icon?
ehthumbs.db

# Windows
Thumbs.db
Thumbs.db:encryptable
ehthumbs_vista.db
*.stackdump
[Dd]esktop.ini
$RECYCLE.BIN/
*.lnk

# Linux (not normally an issue, but some file managers generate these)
.directory
.Trash-*
.fuse_hidden*


# =============================================================================
# 8. Secrets and environment files
# =============================================================================
# This artifact does NOT use any secrets. The .env exclusion is defensive:
# if a future contributor adds a .env for local experimentation, it must
# not be committed. See REPRODUCIBILITY_CHECKLIST.md §9 on the threat
# model (which explicitly states that no credentials are required).

.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
*.crt
secrets.yaml
secrets.yml


# =============================================================================
# 9. Notebook artefacts and Dask scratch
# =============================================================================
# Dask creates a local scheduler directory and a dashboard port file that
# do not belong in the repo.

dask-worker-space/
dask_local_workspace/
*.dask


# =============================================================================
# 10. Docker build artefacts
# =============================================================================
# The Dockerfile is committed. Build artefacts (tar bundles, layer caches
# if a reviewer builds inside the repo directory) are not.

*.dockerignore.local
docker-compose.override.yml


# =============================================================================
# 11. Compiled binaries and native artefacts
# =============================================================================
# Any C extension built in-place by pip (rare on modern wheels, but
# possible on unusual platforms).

*.so
*.o
*.a
*.dylib
*.dll
*.pyd


# =============================================================================
# 12. Optional: PyCharm run configurations
# =============================================================================
# Teams that use PyCharm sometimes commit shared run configs under
# .idea/runConfigurations/. This artifact does not ship any; if a
# reviewer wants to add one, it should be a separate commit, not mixed
# with the artifact.

# !.idea/runConfigurations/
